`getReadModel<T>(name: string): T` is the processor's read path into materialized views the reactor already maintains. It is a name lookup with a cast, and it **throws** when the name is not registered — there is no undefined return to check.

The reactor ships four built-ins:

| Name | Type | What it gives you |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `"document-view"` | `IDocumentView` | Document snapshots for reads: `get()`, `find()`, consistency tracking |
| `"document-indexer"` | `IDocumentIndexer` | Parent/child document relationships and metadata |
| `"processor-manager"` | `IProcessorManager` | Routes operations to registered processors |
| `"subscription-notification"` | `SubscriptionNotificationReadModel` | Notifies subscription callbacks on document change |

Custom read models registered through `IReadModelCoordinator` resolve by their `name` property the same way.

The throwing behaviour drives where you call it. Resolve read models in the **factory**, not inside `onOperations`, so a missing dependency fails loudly at registration rather than on the first operation batch:

```typescript
export const budgetCheckProcessorFactory =
  (module: IProcessorHostModule) =>
  async (driveHeader: PHDocumentHeader): Promise<ProcessorRecord[]> => {
    const budgetModel = module.getReadModel<IBudgetReadModel>("budget");
    const processor = new BudgetCheckProcessor(module.dispatch, budgetModel);
    return [{ processor, filter: { branch: ["main"], documentId: ["*"],
      documentType: ["powerhouse/expense-report"], scope: ["global"] } }];
  };
```

If the read model is genuinely optional, wrap the factory call in try/catch and degrade — the docs call this out explicitly. Note also that in Connect the read models available to `getReadModel` are exactly `reactorModule.readModelCoordinator.readModels`, which is only populated on the in-process reactor path.

Provenance: academy `03-Build/04-WorkWithData/05-ProcessorBestPractices.md` (published), wiring confirmed in `apps/connect/src/store/processor-host-module.ts` at 6.2.2-dev.45.
